'''William Fotheringham''' (born 1965) is a sports writer specialising in [[cycle sport|cycling]] and [[Rugby football|rugby]]. As a newspaper journalist, he writes for ''[[The Guardian]]''.<ref name="guardian">{{cite web| url=https://www.theguardian.com/guardiancontacts/page/0,,505021,00.html| title=Guardian contacts| work=The Guardian}}</ref> Fotheringham was a features editor for ''[[Cycling Weekly]]'', the features editor of ''[[Cycle Sport]]'' and co-founder of ''[[Procycling]]'' magazine.<ref name="guardian" /> He is a current writer for ''[[procycling Magazine]]''.<ref name="rouleur">{{cite web| url=http://rouleur.cc/index.php?option=com_content&view=category&layout=blog&id=54&Itemid=172| title=William Fotheringham| publisher=Rouleur Magazine}}</ref>

A graduate of [[Cambridge University]] with a degree in French, Russian and Italian, Fotheringham won the [[IPC Media]] Specialist Writer of the Year award in 1993 and 1998.<ref name="guardian" />

He has been a racing cyclist for nearly 40 years.<ref name="rouleur" /> He is described by [[Rapha (sportswear)|Rapha]] as being "one of the finest writers in the ‘Pro Tour’ pressroom".<ref>{{cite web| url=http://www.rapha.cc/index.php?page=94| title=Rouleur Magazine| publisher=Rapha}}</ref>

His 2012 biography of [[Eddy Merckx]] ''Half-Man, Half-Bike'', was the first cycling title to achieve No 1 status in the [[The Sunday Times Bestseller List|Sunday Times bestseller lists]].

He is the brother of fellow cycling journalist [[Alasdair Fotheringham]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://rouleurmagazine.wordpress.com/2012/07/04/on-the-shelf/ |title=On the Shelf |last1=Cleverly |first1=Ian |date=4 July 2012 |website=[[Rouleur (magazine)|Rouleur]] |access-date=16 April 2014}}</ref>

His most recent books are Sunday in Hell, an account of how iconic director Jorgen Leth made the film of the same name about the 1976 [[Paris–Roubaix]], and The Greatest, the Times and Life of Beryl Burton, a self-published venture.

